  "origin": "artisyn-harness",
  "operation": "harvest",
  "summary": "Separation-of-concerns fix for the approval pattern split (dev canonical 2.21.1, design canonical 3.4.1), following two rounds of review feedback. Round 1: approval-sign-off.md and approval-intelligent.md mixed concerns (sign-off philosophized about intelligent-approval, both hardcoded a call-site inventory -- a real contradiction, since dev's sign-off pattern listed Plan Step 4 rework as mandatory while intelligent-approval listed the same checkpoint as conditionally automatic). Both patterns are now independent and lean: approval-sign-off.md defines only the sign-off mechanism (three shapes -- content/manual-operation/external-confirmation -- plus the generic ask-only-when-material rules), with zero cross-reference to approval-intelligent.md and zero site enumeration. approval-intelligent.md defines only the auto-approve mechanism, with no 'designated checkpoints' list. Every reference file states its own criterion inline and cites whichever pattern governs it -- extended to every design stop-and-wait point (Research through Publish) for consistency, since design never had a shared pattern to cite before this feature existed. Round 2: fixed 'materiality criterion' polarity (the stated condition described when auto-approval fires, not when human review is required, inverting the pattern's own definition) -- renamed to 'auto-approval conditions' phrased as what must be true to skip review, at all 4 checkpoint sites. Finished extracting lifecycle policy out of approval-sign-off.md into the orchestrator (SKILL.md) and relevant references (do-report-commit.md already owned commit policy; 06-document.md/07-publish.md already owned archival) -- the pattern no longer owns general routine-work authorization, commit policy, or archival scope. Design's approval-intelligent.md no longer hardcodes design/Report.md as the recording destination; the invoking reference now supplies it. Round 3: Ceremony/Frame's auto-approval conditions omitted a case -- 'next status isn't obvious' was an escalation trigger with no corresponding auto-approval condition to fail, so both branches could technically fire together; added 'In Progress is the confirmed, unambiguous next workflow transition' as a third condition.",
  "package_version": "7.27.0",
